                <pb facs="0700" n="618" sameAs="frus:frus1911/pg618"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 812.0441/1.</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The American Consul General at the City of Mexico to
                        the Secretary of State.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >American Consulate General</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Mexico</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1910-11-01"><hi rendition="#italic">November 1,
                        1910</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 453.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to transmit herewith that
                    part of the Diario Oficial of July 22, 1910, which gives the new order to the
                    governors <pb facs="0701" n="619" xml:id="pg619"/> of the several States to
                    permit the consul to visit a prisoner, even though he is “incomunicado.” The
                    Department will appreciate the meaning of this order, because heretofore the law
                    of Mexico has been that a prisoner shall be kept “incommunicado” 72 hours,
                    without being allowed to see and talk with the consul, his family, friends, or
                    lawyer.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.],</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Arnold Shanklin.</hi></signed></closer>


                <xi:include href="d886-a1.xml" xpointer="d886-a1"/>
            </div>
